961	Monte Carlo (MC) modeling of the above processes by LHC experiments typically~\cite{Aad:2015xaa,Khachatryan:2014dka,ATLAS:2015nsi,Khachatryan:2015dcf}
962	involve leading order (LO) simulations matched to parton showers (PS)
963	and are normalized with constant factors, so-called $K$-factors, to account for QCD corrections.
964	
965	While sufficient for predicting total inclusive cross sections,
966	the procedure does not correctly capture the kinematic changes 
967	induced  by high-transverse momentum $(p_T)$
968	initial-state radiation (ISR) or final-state radiation (FSR). 
969	This can substantially impact experimental sensitivity,
970	particularly as $\WR~(\ZR)$ decays to top quarks involve up to four (six) energetic jets at LO.
971	
972	Jet matching schemes at a scale $\mu\ll M_{V_R}$, for $V_{R} = \WR,\ZR$,  can alleviate such problems.
973	However, missing virtual corrections give rise to potentially unstable soft/collinear logarithms of the 
974	form $\alpha_s(\MVR^2)\log(\MVR^2/\mu^{2})$ that spoil perturbative convergence for sufficiently large $(\MVR/\mu)$ ratios.
975	
976	Furthermore, decays of high-mass RH gauge bosons to top quarks and heavy neutrinos can give rise to 
977	top~\cite{Baur:2007ck,Thaler:2008ju,Kaplan:2008ie} and heavy neutrino~\cite{Mitra:2016kov} jets, 
978	which carry different color charges, and hence possess different QCD radiation patterns.
979	Observables sensitive to the structure of these jets, e.g., jet mass, can be used to discriminate against SM backgrounds 
980	but require information that first arises with $\mathcal{O}(\as)$ corrections.
981	
982	
983	To resolve these complications, 
984	we present the automation of next-to-leading-order (NLO) in QCD corrections with parton shower (PS) matching for an 
985	effective LRSM, using the FeynRules (FR) + NLOCT + MadGraph5\_aMC@NLO (\mgamc)~\cite{Alloul:2013bka,Christensen:2008py,Degrande:2014vpa,Alwall:2014hca} 
986	framework.
987	
988	The universal FR object (UFO) files~\cite{Degrande:2011ua} are publicly available from~\cite{nloFRModel} and require remarkably fewer 
989	inputs for simulating fully differential, benchmark collider processes 
990	than current LO implementations~\cite{Sjostrand:2006za,Ashry:2013loa,Roitgrund:2014zka}.
991	
992	We demonstrate this by providing predictions for $\WR,\ZR$ production at the $\sqrt{s} = 13$ TeV LHC 
993	and a future 100 TeV Very Large Hadron Collider (VLHC)~\cite{Arkani-Hamed:2015vfh}.
994	We also present predictions for inclusive $N$ production at a hypothetical Large Hadron Electron Collider (LHeC)~\cite{AbelleiraFernandez:2012cc}.
995	The Born diagrams for these processes are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dydisProd}.
996	
997	As a case study, we investigate at NLO+PS accuracy the properties of 
998	heavy neutrino (color-singlet) jets and top quark (color-triplet) jets from decays of high-mass $\WR$ bosons at the LHC.
999	
