1151	\caption{Masses and total widths of $\WR,~\ZR$ and $N_1$ for representative parameters in Eq.(\ref{eq:lrsmInputs}).}
1152	\label{tb:lrsmParam}
1153	\end{center}
1154	\end{table}
1155	
1156	While we take $\MWR$ and $\MZR$ as independent phenomenological parameters, they are related in the LRSM by the relation
1157	\begin{equation}
1158	 \MZR = \sqrt{2\cos^2\theta_W / \cos2\theta_W} \times \MWR \approx (1.7)\times\MWR
1159	 \label{eq:massRelationship}
1160	\end{equation}
1161	As the size of $m_{N_{m'}}$ are governed by Yukawa couplings, the masses of $N_{m'}$ are largely independent of $\MWR$.
1162	For the following representative input, 
1163	\begin{equation}
1164	 \MWR=3\TeV,	\quad m_{N_1}=m_t=173.3\GeV,	\quad\text{and}\quad  m_{N_2},m_{N_3} = 10^{12}\GeV,
1165	 \label{eq:lrsmInputs}
1166	\end{equation}
1167	which we will motivate in the next section, the corresponding partial and total widths for $\WR,\ZR,$ and $N_1$ are summarized in Tb.~\ref{tb:lrsmParam}.
1168	We have checked our model against these analytic results.
1169	
1170	
1171	
1172	
1173	
1174	
1175	
1176	\subsection{Collider Constraints on Effective LRSM}\label{sec:constrains}
1177	Direct and indirect tests place stringent limits on the LRSM. 
1178	For a recent review, see~\cite{Mitra:2016kov} and references therein.
1179	Current LHC dijet and dileptons+jets searches require ~\cite{Aad:2015xaa,Khachatryan:2014dka,ATLAS:2015nsi,Khachatryan:2015dcf}:
1180	\begin{equation}
1181	 \MWR > 2.6-2.7\TeV \quad\text{at}\quad 95\% \quad\text{CL~for}\quad\kappa_{R}^{q,\ell}=1.
1182	\end{equation}
1183	Using the $\MWR-\MZR$ mass relation of Eq.~(\ref{eq:massRelationship}), the subsequently limit on $\MZR$ is: 
1184	\begin{equation}
1185	 \MZR > 4.4-4.6\TeV \quad\text{at}\quad 95\% \quad\text{CL~for}\quad\kappa_{R}^{q,\ell}=1.
1186	\end{equation}
1187	Heavy neutrino masses remain unconstrained at colliders for $(m_{N_{m'}} / \MWR) \lesssim 0.1$ 
1188	due a breakdown of standard collider searches~\cite{Mitra:2016kov}.
1189	
1190	We exploit this latitude and equate the lightest heavy neutrino mass, which we denote for simplicity as $N$ with mass $m_N$, to the top quark mass, $m_t$.
1191	For simplicity, we decouple the two remaining heavy neutrinos with unrealistically large masses.
1192	We do not advocate such a scenario will be realized in nature.
1193	This mass assignment permits us to make a more systematic comparison of heavy neutrino and top jets in Sec.~\ref{sec:jetKin}.
1194	
1195	We summarize our choices of LRSM inputs in Eq.~(\ref{eq:lrsmInputs}).
1196	
